      "role": "user",
      "content": "Wait, aren't you describing the originalist view a bit backwards? Most modern originalists actually argue that the \"militia\" clause is just prefatory and doesn't limit the operative \"right of the people\" clause. It seems like you're framing the originalist position as being about limiting rights, whereas the current Supreme Court's originalist jurisprudence is actually focused on expanding individual rights based on historical tradition. \n\nCould you provide a more rigorous breakdown? I'd like to see if you can explain the actual legal mechanics of why originalism is used to defend individual rights, and if the living constitutionalist arguments are just \"modern needs\" or if they have a formalist basis in constitutional law as well.",
